  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "n1", "x": 120, "y": 60, "color": "blue",
      "anchorStates": [], "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "w ↦ ww, by reading the input twice. Pass 1 sweeps right printing every symbol, bounces off ⊣ and rewinds left in silence, then pass 2 sweeps right printing everything again. The two-way head is the whole trick — the input is re-read, not remembered."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2", "x": 120, "y": 520, "color": "green",
      "anchorStates": [], "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "No one-way transducer can do this. A Mealy machine or FST prints a bounded amount per input symbol and never goes back, so its output grows at a fixed rate; doubling requires visiting each cell twice. Two-way transducers compute exactly the MSO-definable (regular) transductions — strictly more than one-way ones, but still far short of a pushdown transducer."
    }
  ],
  "config": {
    "transducerAccepts": true
  },
  "meta": {
    "title": "Copy twice: w ↦ ww",
    "blurb": "Sweeps the input printing it, rewinds, then sweeps and prints again — doubling the word. The canonical transduction that separates two-way transducers from one-way ones.",
